==English==
{{wikipedia|lang=en}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|colloïde|lang=fr}}, from {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|κόλλα||glue|tr=kolla|lang=grc}} + {{term|-oid|lang=en}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈkɒlɔɪd/|lang=en}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# [[glue|Glue]]-like; [[gelatinous]].
#: '''''colloid''' tumours''

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|chemistry|lang=en}} A [[stable]] system of two [[phase]]s, one of which is [[dispersed]] in the other in the form of very small [[droplet]]s or [[particle]]s.
# {{context|meteorology|lang=en}} An [[intimate]] [[mixture]] of two [[substances]] one of which, called the [[dispersed]] phase (or '''colloid'''), is uniformly distributed in a finely divided state throughout the second substance, called the [[dispersion]] medium (or dispersing medium). The dispersion medium may be a [[gas]], a [[liquid]], or a [[solid]], and the dispersed phase may also be any of these, with the exception that one does not speak of a colloidal system of one gas in another. A system of liquid or solid particles colloidally dispersed in a gas is called an [[aerosol]]. A system of solid substances or water-[[insoluble]] liquids colloidally dispersed in liquid water is called a [[hydrosol]].
# {{context|geology|lang=en}} A [[particle]] less than 1 [[micron]] in [[diameter]], following the [[Wentworth scale]]

